The test first constructs `Conv2D(...)`, so execution starts in `Conv2D.__init__`, which immediately delegates to `BaseConv.__init__`. When `layer(x)` is evaluated on an unbuilt layer, Keras builds it first, so `BaseConv.build` runs and calls `compute_output_shape` inside `build` before creating weights. The forward pass then enters `BaseConv.call`, which accesses the `kernel` property, calls `convolution_op`, and that delegates through `ops.conv` to the TensorFlow backend `nn.conv`; inside `nn.conv`, the actual convolution is performed by the nested `_conv`, which first calls `_convert_data_format`. After convolution and bias addition, `BaseConv.call` applies the resolved activation function; because the activation is `"relu"`, `activations.relu` calls `ReLU.static_call`, which in the default-parameter branch invokes backend `nn.relu`.

